percycle is genuinely\n  structural, with multi-year backlog cover through FY2028.\n\nReason for not selecting Engine D/E/F: no meaningful recurring\nplatform revenue; no single binary approval event; current earnings\nare substantive.\n\nKey conclusion up front: Expected Intrinsic Value \u2248 $105 per share\n(range: $30 very-bear \u2013 $210 bull). T0 price = $190.63.\nMargin of Safety = \u221281.5% (none). OVERVALUED.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro variable check:\n- 10Y Treasury yield: 4.65\u20134.71% [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/YCharts,\n  2026-08-21]. Up ~0.44pt YoY. Direction: sideways-to-rising.\n- Real interest rates: elevated; 10Y nominal ~4.7% vs inflation\n  above target \u2192 real ~2.2-2.5% [Estimate].\n- DXY: 98.6\u201399.9 [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics, 2026-08-20].\n  Direction: mild downtrend.\n- Fed stance: HOLD; Chair Warsh, FOMC divided, inflation above 2%\n  (tariffs + Middle East conflict). No near-term rate cuts\n  [Actual \u2014 FOMC minutes, Kitco 2026-08-19; Reuters 2026-08-14].\n- Global liquidity: tight-ish; QT still in process; Big Tech\n  spending is a private-sector liquidity offset [Estimate].\n- Economic cycle: late expansion; inflation stickiness; labor\n  constraints.\n- Inflation: above 2%; commodities (copper/steel) moderately rising\n  [Actual \u2014 company Q3 FY2026 call: \"moderate inflation on core\n  commodities\"].\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed \u2014 search budget exhausted; not\n  established from filings]. Treated as neutral-to-tight in late\n  cycle; flag for monitoring.\n- US fiscal: large deficits ongoing; capital spending (IRA/CHIPS)\n  remains a tailwind for grid investment.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime classification (probability distribution):\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 10%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 55%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 25%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 10%\nTotal = 100%. Base regime: REFLATION with a meaningful stagflation\ntail. Rationale: 10Y ~4.7%, inflation above target, but economy\nstill growing with record AI/capex investment.\n\nStep 1-3. POWL macro sensitivity (each -3 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: \u22122 (utilities are rate-sensitive\n  capex; higher discount rate pressures equity value).\n- Dollar sensitivity: \u22121 (only ~20% international revenue; weaker\n  dollar mildly positive).\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (data-center/utility capex are\n  well-funded by Big Tech cash flows).\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: \u22122 (project capex is deferrable).\n- Commodity sensitivity: \u22122 (copper, aluminum, steel input costs).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment judgment:\nRegime-weighted impact: Reflation 55% \u00d7 slightly negative (-2) +\nStagflation 25% \u00d7 negative (-3) + Goldilocks 10% \u00d7 positive (+1) +\nRecession 10% \u00d7 negative (-2) = \u22121.1 \u2212 0.75 + 0.1 \u2212 0.2 = \u22121.95.\n\u2192 HEADWIND (macro).\nConviction \u22120.5 steps; strengthen split-entry / entry discipline.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Similar-enterprise base rates:\n- Project-engineered capital goods company surviving 10 years:\n  >95%.\n- Probability that a cyclical at 6x its historical median OM retains\n  peak margins for >5 years: <25% [Assumption \u2014 based on mean\n  reversion in industrial project businesses].\n- Probability of >40% drawdown within 3 years of a cyclical peak:\n  30-40% (framework reference base rate).\n- Probability that a TAM narrative (data center electrification)\n  delivers 15%+ growth for 5+ years: ~20-30% (framework base rate).\n- Large capacity expansion (greenfield $70-100M) delivering ROIC >\n  WACC within 5 years: ~50-60% (base rate for organic capacity adds\n  in a capacity-short market).\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate; justification:\nThe analysis assumes POWL can beat the \"margins regress\" base rate\nby holding 16-19.5% OM for 5 years and sustaining 8-20% revenue\ngrowth. Justifications (3+):\n1. Backlog of $2.4B (record; 2.0x FY25 revenue) provides revenue and\n   gross-margin visibility through FY2028 \u2014 unlike the company's\n   historical book-and-bill model.\n2. The demand drivers are structural: US data-center power demand\n   forecast 75.8GW in 2026 \u2192 134.4GW by 2030 [Estimate \u2014 research\n   brief citing industry projections]; Eaton data-center orders\n   +85% YoY, backlog +33% [Actual \u2014 Morgan Stanley via Fool].\n3. The business mix has permanently shifted from commodity\n   petrochemical projects to utility/data-center systems with higher\n   specification content and longer design cycles, supporting\n   structurally higher gross margins.\n4. Zero-debt balance sheet with $634M liquidity [Estimate \u2014 company\n   statement Q3 FY2026 call] removes balance-sheet cyclical risk.\nEven so, margins are assumed to fade from 19.5% to 16.5% by FY2030\nin the base case \u2014 a deliberate deviation from the 2.9% historical\nmedian, but not a permanence of the current peak.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:\n- Success probability implied by price (margin permanence + 20%\n  growth for a decade) vs base rate (20-30%): implied >50%.\n  \u2192 Ratio > 1.5x. Additional evidence required. RE-EVALUATION FLAG:\n  the market price embeds a materially better-than-base-rate world.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):\nPowell Industries designs and manufactures custom-engineered\nmedium- and low-voltage electrical distribution and control\nsystems (switchgear, power control rooms, motor control centers)\nfor electric utilities, data centers, LNG/gas processing, and\nheavy industry. The company is a project business: it bids\nengineered-to-order contracts, fabricates at US Gulf Coast sites,\nrecognizes revenue primarily under percentage-of-completion, and\nholds a record $2.4B backlog providing visibility through FY2028.\nIt also provides aftermarket services and field support, with a\nrecently acquired Remsdaq automation capability integrated into\ndata-center offerings.\nUnderstandability rating: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment:\n- Intangible assets (brand/engineering reputation): 2/3\n- Switching costs: 2/3\n- Network effects: 0/3\n- Cost advantage: 1/3\n- Economies of scale: 2/3\n- Regulatory barriers: 1/3\n- Data moat: 0/3\nTotal: 8/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT.\nMoat direction: WIDENING vs 3 years ago \u2014 market-share gains in\ndata centers/utilities, delivery speed as a differentiator,\nintegrated automation content (Remsdaq) lifts pricing power,\ncapacity expansion while competitors are capacity-constrained.\nNarrowing risks: ABB/Siemens/Eaton pushing into medium-voltage\nswitchgear, commodity input cost pass-through limited by fixed\ncontracts.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification:\nCYCLICAL with GROWTH CHARACTERISTICS. It is not a \"fast grower\" in\nthe classic sense (earnings are not secular), but the backlog and\ndata-center/utility supercycle give it rapid-growth optics. Key\nLynch checkpoints: Cyclicals should be bought at high P/E/low\nearnings and sold at low P/E/high earnings. On peak earnings\n(FY2026E EPS ~$5.52), the stock trades at 34.5x forward earnings,\nbut on peak-cycle basis that is NOT a buy signal. Classified\nCyclical \u2192 valuation discipline strict.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:\n- Revenue growth: 3Y CAGR (FY22\u2192FY25) +27.4%; 5Y CAGR +16.3%\n  [Arithmetic on filed data].\n- Operating margin: FY2025 19.7%; 3-yr avg (FY23-25) 15.5%;\n  12-yr median 2.9%. Verdict: peak.\n- EPS growth: FY2024\u2192FY2025 +20.7% (0.150\u21920.181 NI on flat share\n  count); FY2026 TTM +~10% [Estimate].\n- ROE: 28.3% [Aggregator]; ROIC GAAP: >100% [Estimate \u2014 NOPAT\n  $170M / (equity + debt \u2212 cash \u2212 ST inv) $165M]. Verdict:\n  extraordinary, but influenced by project-liability float.\n- FCF margin / conversion: FY2025 FCF $155M / NI $181M = 86%\n  conversion; FCF margin ~14%. TTM: $244M / $191M = 128%.\n- Net debt/EBITDA: \u22122.8x (net CASH) [Estimate].\n- Interest coverage: effectively infinite (zero debt).\nOverall: EXCELLENT balance sheet; PROFIT QUALITY at cyclical peak.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard:\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 (no meaningful buybacks; share count\n   flat; SBC small).\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 (Remsdaq integration adds automation\n   content; no impairments; small scale).\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 (current capex minimal; Jacintoport\n   expected >$100M incremental revenue; greenfield decision pending\n   \u2014 unproven).\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 (prudent capex + zero debt + cash pile\n   growing; but $634M cash earning ~4% is suboptimal).\n5. Guidance accuracy: 1/3 (Q1 FY2026 EPS missed consensus; Q3\n   FY2026 missed by ~$0.05).\nInsider bonus: CEO holding likely >1% but <5% [Unconfirmed];\nrecent insider selling (CEO 10b5-1 plan, Director Williams sold\n2,250 shares 2026-08-14 [Estimate \u2014 GuruFocus]) \u2192 \u22122.\nTotal: 8 + 0 \u2212 2 = 8/17. ADEQUATE-TO-GOOD, not excellent.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n2.5-1. R&D capitalization: R&D not separately disclosed; included\nin SG&A (FY2025 SG&A $95M / 8.6% of revenue). No material\nadjustment [Assumption \u2014 R&D-like engineering embedded in project\ncosts is expensed in COGS; qualifying as a real feature, not a\ndistortion].\n2.5-2. SBC treatment: FY2025 SBC = $5M (0.45% of revenue). True\nFCF adjustment: Reported FCF $155M \u2212 SBC $5M = $150M owner FCF.\nTTM: $244M \u2212 ~$5M = $239M. Minor drag.\n2.5-3. Operating leases: $2M (FY2025). Negligible; adds ~$40M to\ndebt equivalents if capitalized 20x \u2014 still net cash positive.\n2.5-4. Brand/data assets: none material.\n2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: NOPAT $170M (22% tax) / Invested capital\n(equity $641 + debt $0 \u2212 cash $451 \u2212 ST inv $25 = $165M) \u2248 103%.\nGAAP ROIC is ~100%+. Gap <5pp \u2192 no adjustment needed. The high\nROIC is real but reflects (a) leveraged float from customer\nprogress billings (liabilities $468M) and (b) peak margins.\n2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett):\nFY2025: NI $181 + D&A $7 \u2212 maintenance capex ~$8 [Estimate] \u2212\n\u0394WC drag $137 [Arithmetic] = $43M.\nTTM: $191 + ~$7 \u2212 $8 \u2212 (WC release ~$65) = ~$295M [Estimate].\nOwner-earnings are extremely volatile: FY2025 ~$43M, TTM ~$295M.\nUsing OCF\u2212capex\u2212SBC = $150M FY2025, $239M TTM. The midpoint for\nvaluation should be ~$150-180M normalised owner FCF.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION ENGINE (Blended)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDiscount rate derivation (WACC):\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.70% [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics 2026-08-21].\n- Equity risk premium: 4.7% [Assumption \u2014 standard long-run ERP].\n- Beta: 1.216 [Aggregator \u2014 vendor window unstated, low trust; use\n  as directional].\n- Cost of equity = 4.70% + 1.216 \u00d7 4.70% = 10.4% \u2192 round 10.5%.\n- Zero debt \u2192 WACC \u2248 CoE = 10.5% (no debt tax shield).\n- For cyclical/small/mid-cap project risk, a 10.5-11.5% range is\n  appropriate; sensitivity shown below.\n\nTax rate: 22% [Assumption \u2014 effective tax inputs in pack are\nprovably broken for FY2014; could not retrieve the 10-K before\nbudget exhaustion. Typical US federal+state for an industrial\nwith FDII/R&D credits \u2248 20-24%.]\n\n================================================================\n3-A. EXPLICIT FORECAST (BASE CASE)\n================================================================\nSOURCING OF EVERY FORECAST DRIVER:\n- FY2026 revenue: $1.195B [Estimate] = Q1 0.251 + Q2 0.297 + Q3\n  0.312 [Actual, filed] + Q4 0.335 [Assumption \u2014 record backlog,\n  normal seasonality; consensus \u2248$1.20B].\n- FY2027 revenue: $1.50B [Estimate] \u2014 supported by $1.3B of\n  backlog converting within 12 months (company statement) plus new\n  orders; +25% YoY. Consensus forward growth: revenue +19.5%\n  [Aggregator \u2014 horizon unspecified].\n- FY2028 revenue: $1.75B [Assumption] \u2014 +17%; backlog provides\n  coverage \"through FY2028\" (company); industry demand still\n  expanding (US data-center power demand +77% 2026\u21922030).\n- FY2029 revenue: $1.95B [Assumption] \u2014 +11%; deceleration as\n  cycle normalizes.\n- FY2030 revenue: $2.10B [Assumption] \u2014 +8%; not guided beyond\n  FY2028; deliberately conservative.\n- Operating margin path: FY2026 19.5% [Estimate \u2014 TTM OM 19.7%;\n  closeouts +100bp, offset by inflation]; FY2027 19.0%\n  [Assumption]; FY2028 18.5%; FY2029 17.5%; FY2030 16.5%\n  [Assumption \u2014 fade toward mid-cycle as competition enters].\n  Note: 12-yr median OM is 2.9%; even the terminal 16.5% is a\n  bullish structural assumption.\n- D&A: $9M\u2192$20M [Assumption \u2014 tracking PP&E growth and capacity\n  investments].\n- Capex: FY2026 $30M, FY2027 $55M, FY2028 $65M (greenfield\n  $70-100M partially spent within 2027-28 [Unconfirmed \u2014 mgmt is\n  \"evaluating\"; assume 50% probability-weighted build]), FY2029\n  $45M, FY2030 $35M [Assumptions]. TTM capex is only $12M.\n- \u0394Working capital: positive = USE of cash. FY2025 \u0394WC was +$137M\n  use; TTM saw a ~$65M release. Assume builds resume with growth:\n  FY2026 $40M, FY2027 $40M, FY2028 $25M, FY2029 $10M, FY2030 $5M\n  [Assumptions].\n- Terminal growth: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 \u2264 nominal GDP; project\n  businesses rarely sustain above-GDP growth forever].\n- WACC: 10.5% [derived above].\n\nKEY ASSUMPTION INTEGRITY: Beyond FY2026, NONE of the revenue,\nmargin, capex or WC path is guided by the company. Management\nguidance covers only capacity milestones and qualitative demand\n(record backlog, visibility through FY2028). A 10-year trend\nextension would be an accident; the fade to 16.5% OM and +8%\ngrowth is a judgment call. Sensitivity to the plausible range is\nshown in 3-C.\n\nBASE-CASE CASH FLOW TABLE ($M, fiscal years ending Sept 30):\n| FY | Rev | EBIT% | EBIT | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | FCF |\n|-----|--------|-------|--------|-------|-----|-------|-----|------|\n| 2026| 1,195 | 19.5% | 233 | 182 | 9 | 30 | 40 | 121 |\n| 2027| 1,500 | 19.0% | 285 | 222 | 11 | 55 | 40 | 138 |\n| 2028| 1,750 | 18.5% | 324 | 252 | 14 | 65 | 25 | 176 |\n| 2029| 1,950 | 17.5% | 341 | 266 | 15 | 45 | 10 | 226 |\n| 2030| 2,100 | 16.5% | 347 | 270 | 16 | 40 | 5 | 241 |\n\n(NOPAT = EBIT \u00d7 (1 \u2212 22%). FCF = NOPAT + D&A \u2212 Capex \u2212 \u0394WC.)\n\nPV @ WACC 10.5% (mid-year convention; DF 0.951, 0.860, 0.778,\n0.704, 0.637):\nStage PV = 121\u00d70.951 + 138\u00d70.860 + 176\u00d70.778 + 226\u00d70.704 +\n241\u00d70.637\n= 115.1 + 118.7 + 136.9 + 159.1 + 153.5 = $683M.\nTerminal value (FY2030 FCF \u00d7 1.03 / (0.105 \u2212 0.03)) =\n241\u00d71.03/0.075 = $3,310M.\nPV of TV = 3,310 \u00d7 0.637 = $2,109M.\nEnterprise value = 683 + 2,109 = $2,792M.\nNet cash + ST investments = $634M [Estimate \u2014 company-stated Q3\nFY2026: \"$634M liquidity\", zero debt; consistent with FY2025\nfiled cash $451M + ST inv $25M plus FY2026 YTD earnings].\nEquity value = $3,426M.\nPer share = $3,426M / 36.43M (post-split shares) = $94.0.\n\n3-B. CYCLE-ADJUSTED CROSS-CHECK (Engine 2):\n- Mid-cycle revenue (FY2029 point in the cycle): ~$1.9B\n  [Estimate].\n- Mid-cycle OM: 13% [Assumption \u2014 midpoint between 12-yr median\n  2.9% and peak 19.7%; generous].\n- Mid-cycle EPS: 1.9B \u00d7 0.13 \u00d7 0.78 / 36.43M = $5.29.\n- Normalized P/E for a cyclical project manufacturer at mid-cycle:\n  12-15x; even at an aggressive 18x: 18 \u00d7 $5.29 = $95.\n- Plus net cash per share $17.40 \u2192 $112.\n- P/B cross-check: 10.3x P/B on 28% ROE implies r \u2248 6.3%, which\n  is implausibly low for a cyclical small/mid-cap. Fair P/B for\n  this risk level (r=10.5%, g=3%, ROE 20% mid-cycle) = 2.3x;\n  BV/share $18.5 \u2192 $42 + net cash $17 = $59 [Estimate].\nEngine 2 range: $59-$112. Midpoint ~$85. Consistent with DCF.\n\n3-C. TORNADO SENSITIVITY (Top 3 variables):\n1. Terminal/normalized OM: 16.5% \u2192 if 12%: IV \u2248 $65; if 20%:\n   IV \u2248 $125.\n2. WACC: 10.5% \u2192 if 9.0%: IV \u2248 $125; if 12.0%: IV \u2248 $75.\n3. FY2028-30 growth path: if +15%/+12%/+9% (vs base +17%/+11%/+8%):\n   IV \u2248 $90; if +25%/+20%/+15%: IV \u2248 $110.\n\n3-D. REVERSE DCF (what the market is pricing at $190.63):\nUsing WACC 10.5%, g 3%, net cash $17.40/share, the implied\nFY2030 (terminal-year) FCF = ~$600-650M and implied FY2030\nrevenue \u2248 $3.0-3.5B at a 19-20% FCF margin, or revenue ~$4.5B at\na 15% FCF margin. To get there from $1.2B in 4 years requires a\n35-45% CAGR \u2014 unprecedented for this business and far beyond the\n\"visibility through FY2028\" backlog. Verdict: UNREALISTIC.\n\nConclusion of Engine selection:\n- Blended IV base = $90-95 (DCF + cycle-adjusted midpoint).\n- Full scenario expected value = $105 (Section 4).\n- Point estimate used for MoS: $105 [Estimate].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3.5-1. Competitive response simulation: If POWL proves out the\ndata-center behind-the-meter model at scale, Eaton, ABB, Siemens,\nand Schneider will attack with vertical-integrated offerings and\npricing pressure (effect: \u22122 to \u22123 on margins by FY2029-30).\nDelivery speed is today's ordering criterion; that advantage erodes\nas competitors add capacity. Base case already reflects this via\nOM fade.\n3.5-2. Reflexivity (Soros): POSITIVE cycle is live \u2014 the high\nstock price funds SBC-based talent attraction, and the strong\nbalance sheet wins customer trust; POWL is a preferred supplier in\ncapacity-constrained times. NEGATIVE loop risk: if the stock\ncontinues falling (already \u221242% from $328 high), talent/labor\nattraction softens and customers may worry about capacity\ndelivery. Net: mildly positive today, fragile.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution: The early adopter (data\ncenter developers) \u2192 mainstream (utilities, hyperscalers) \u2192\nlate adopter (industrial/other) curve is at the early-to-mainstream\npoint. LTV/CAC: CAC is high (custom engineering), but repeat\norders from utilities have been strong (FY2026 utility revenue\n+18-35% YoY). As penetration grows, data-center orders commoditize\nand pricing power fades.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPrior: base rates from Section 1.5 (margin regression likely;\ncycle longevity unlikely beyond 5 years).\nBayesian update with evidence: (1) record $2.4B backlog, (2)\nbook-to-bill 2.2x FYTD, (3) Eaton data-center orders +85%,\n(4) hyperscaler capex \u2265$640B CY2026 \u2014 updated likelihood raises\nthe bull case from 15% to 20%; base case 45%; bear + very bear 35%.\n\nScenario table (posterior probabilities):\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue FY2029 | OM FY2029 | FCF path | IV/share |\n|----------|------|----------------|-----------|----------|----------|\n| Bull     | 20%  | $2.6B          | 20%       | strong, WC release | $210 |\n| Base     | 45%  | $1.95B         | 17.5%     | fade as modeled | $95 |\n| Bear     | 25%  | $1.1B          | 7%        | backlog normalizes, margins collapse | $60 |\n| Very Bear| 10%  | $0.9B          | 3%        | recession + execution failure | $30 |\n\nExpected price = 0.20\u00d7210 + 0.45\u00d795 + 0.25\u00d760 + 0.10\u00d730\n= 42 + 42.75 + 15 + 3 = $102.75 \u2192 round to $105.\n50% CI: $75 \u2013 $175. 80% CI: $30 \u2013 $210.\n\nMargin of safety: (105 \u2212 190.63) / 105 = \u221281.5% \u2192 NONE.\nJudgment: do not invest at $190.63.\n\n4-3. Bayesian note: The bull scenario is at the upper bound of the\nbase-rate prior (20%); the \"supercycle persists\" evidence partially\njustifies it, but the 56% gap between price and even the bull case\nIV highlights how much good news is already priced.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: at ~34.5x forward EPS (FY2026E) and ~30x\nFY2027E, the market is pricing 7-10 years of sustained 20%+ growth\nand near-peak margins. My analysis horizon: 3-5 years.\nSpread = 3-5 years' length (market horizon longer than mine).\nNo positive arbitrage for a long investor.\nFor a holder: the 3-5 year risk/reward is asymmetric to the\ndownside. SHORT-side opportunity may exist, but is not part of the\nframework's authorization.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nConviction Score (15 pts):\n1. Business quality: 2/3\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 0/3\n3. Macro alignment: 1/3\n4. Thesis clarity: 3/3\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3\nTotal: 8/15 \u2192 MEDIUM conviction.\nTransition penalty (Archetype C\u2192B): \u22120.5 steps \u2192 MEDIUM-LOW.\nConviction decay (\u22121 per 6 months without confirming evidence):\nnot applied since thesis is current.\nPosition sizing guideline: 0-4% maximum for medium-low conviction;\nwith MoS negative, the correct tactical weight is 0%.\nDollar-neutral guidance: NO POSITION.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Short % float: 11.1% [Actual \u2014 Aggregator 2026-08-16].\n  Elevated; days to cover 3.48; MoM change +22.3% \u2192 shorts\n  ADDING. Squeeze potential exists on good news.\n- Institutional ownership: 94.23% [Aggregator \u2014 13F lagged].\n  High; stock is exposed to passive flows and ETF selling on\n  momentum breaks.\n- Insider ownership: 20.63% [Aggregator]; CEO has sold under a\n  10b5-1 plan; Director R. Williams sold 2,250 shares on\n  2026-08-14 [Estimate \u2014 GuruFocus]. Insider cluster = negative\n  signal.\n- Options positioning: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not in pack/search budget].\n  P/C skew unknown; note gap.\n- Sentiment: stock \u221242% from 52-week high ($328); sell-side mean\n  target $280 (30% upside), median $276 [Aggregator]. Momentum\n  broken; analyst optimism sticky.\n- Crowding risk score: 8/15 (high ownership + high short interest\n  + crowded data-center/AI trade). If long, \u22121% weight penalty.\nMOST IMPORTANT BEHAVIORAL READ: the market is paying a large\npremium to the consensus narrative; the crowded side is LONG.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- POWL is a high-beta (1.22) cyclical exposed to AI data-center\n  capex, US grid investment, and industrial electrification.\n  In a \"Reflation\" regime it is an equity-beta beneficiary, but\n  with negative macro alignment score (\u22121.95) it should not be an\n  overweight.\n- Correlation with existing portfolio: if book holds other data-\n  center/electrification longs (ETN, VRT, NVDA, etc.), POWL adds\n  concentrated factor risk.\n- With a negative expected return (\u221246% to expected IV) and\n  crowded positioning, POWL does not fit the current book as a\n  new position. If portfolio is underweight electrification, a\n  starter position size of 1% is the maximum acceptable (only\n  upon a pullback to \u2264$150).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nUsing scenario probabilities and IVs:\nExpected return = \u03a3 p_i \u00d7 (IV_i \u2212 Price)/Price:\n0.20\u00d7(210\u2212190.63)/190.63 + 0.45\u00d7(95\u2212190.63)/190.63 +\n0.25\u